@meta
id: 14-12
title: Look first, then replace
chapter: 14
type: lesson
skills: sub-basic, sub-confirm
par: 36
minutes: 5
vscode: Ctrl-F, Enter a few times to look, then switch the box to Replace
open: payment.service.ts

@brief
`refundLegacy` is being retired. Its replacement, `refundGateway`,
has the same signature and the same `.check` helper, so every use in
this service changes name and nothing else.

You know two ways to be sure a rename is safe: the `c` flag, or
reading the hits first. This lesson is the second one. Put the cursor
on the identifier, use the star search from chapter 12 to light up
every occurrence, and step through them with `n` until you have seen
them all. Then run the substitute — with an empty pattern.

An empty pattern is not a mistake. It means "the pattern I just
searched for".

@keys
| `*` | search for the word under the cursor, whole-word, and set the last search pattern |
| `n` | next match — look at it |
| `:%s//new/g` `Enter` | empty pattern: reuse whatever the last search was |
| `2w` | from `import` onto the identifier |

@hints
The last search pattern is a thing vim remembers, and `:s` can read it.
Leave the pattern part of the substitute empty.
--
`*` on `refundLegacy` sets the pattern to `\<refundLegacy\>` — anchors
included, for free. `n` walks the four hits. Then `:%s//refundGateway/g`.
--
`2w` puts the cursor on the identifier in the import. `*`, then `n`
three times to see the rest, then `:%s//refundGateway/g` and Enter.

@solution
    2w  *  n n n  :%s//refundGateway/g<Enter>  :wq<Enter>

Thirty-one keystrokes, including three spent looking, which is the
part that matters.

Two things are being taught. The first is the workflow: search, walk
the hits with `n`, and only then substitute. The `c` flag asks you at
each match; this asks you once, up front, with the whole file
available to scroll. They suit different moods. Confirm is better when
the hits are many and mixed; look-then-replace is better when they
are few and you want to see the code around each one before deciding
on the replacement at all.

The second is the empty pattern. `:s//x/` reuses the last search
pattern, whatever set it: `/`, `?`, `*`, `#`, or a previous `:s`. It
saves typing, but the real gain is that you substitute exactly what
you looked at. If you had searched `/refundLeg` to save keystrokes and
then run `:%s//refundGateway/g`, you would have `refundGatewayacy`
four times over. What you saw is what you change; make sure what you
saw was the whole word.

@start payment.service.ts
import { refundLegacy } from './legacy/refund';
import { injectable } from 'tsyringe';
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';

@injectable()
export class PaymentService {
  public async refund(orderId: string, amount: number) {
    const response = await refundLegacy(orderId, amount);

    if (response.status !== 200) {
      throw exception('REFUND_FAILED');
    }

    return response.data;
  }

  public async refundBatch(orderIds: string[], amount: number) {
    const responses = await Promise.all(orderIds.map((orderId) => refundLegacy(orderId, amount)));
    const failed = responses.filter((response) => response.status !== 200);

    if (failed.length > 0) {
      throw exception('REFUND_FAILED');
    }

    return responses.map((response) => response.data);
  }

  public async canRefund(orderId: string) {
    const eligibility = await refundLegacy.check(orderId);

    return eligibility.allowed;
  }
}

@expected payment.service.ts
import { refundGateway } from './legacy/refund';
import { injectable } from 'tsyringe';
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';

@injectable()
export class PaymentService {
  public async refund(orderId: string, amount: number) {
    const response = await refundGateway(orderId, amount);

    if (response.status !== 200) {
      throw exception('REFUND_FAILED');
    }

    return response.data;
  }

  public async refundBatch(orderIds: string[], amount: number) {
    const responses = await Promise.all(orderIds.map((orderId) => refundGateway(orderId, amount)));
    const failed = responses.filter((response) => response.status !== 200);

    if (failed.length > 0) {
      throw exception('REFUND_FAILED');
    }

    return responses.map((response) => response.data);
  }

  public async canRefund(orderId: string) {
    const eligibility = await refundGateway.check(orderId);

    return eligibility.allowed;
  }
}
